00:11:32.992 --> 00:11:37.530
that required access to structured data
about the deals, web data

235
00:11:37.863 --> 00:11:41.133
about any regulatory changes
happening in the industries

236
00:11:41.133 --> 00:11:44.136
that these companies operate
in, along with meeting mode access.

237
00:11:44.203 --> 00:11:47.540
To understand our value proposition
in each of these deals.

238
00:11:47.707 --> 00:11:51.410
Here we can see the response
from the agent to this question.

239
00:11:51.477 --> 00:11:56.477
The agent was unsuccessful in actually
reasoning about this complicated request.

240
00:11:56.615 --> 00:12:00.386
It provided some information about how
it might go about accomplishing the task.

241
00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:03.355
Couldn't actually provide the key details
that we need.

242
00:12:03.355 --> 00:12:04.857
In the next lesson, we'll be able to learn

243
00:12:04.857 --> 00:12:07.126
more about what actually went wrong here.

244
00:12:07.126 --> 00:12:09.028
here's
the third example that I already ran.

245
00:12:09.028 --> 00:12:11.997
Here we asked for a common theme
across our different meeting notes.

246
00:12:11.997 --> 00:12:14.633
This required access
to the unstructured data,

247
00:12:14.633 --> 00:12:17.636
And the synthesizer
was able to provide us a theme

248
00:12:17.837 --> 00:12:21.173
that the meeting notes focused on aligning
sales efforts with client needs.

249
00:12:21.273 --> 00:12:24.643
In the next lesson,
you'll use tracing to really understand

250
00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:28.614
exactly what happens under the hood
when the agent is executing.

251
00:12:28.814 --> 00:12:29.715
You'll also start

252
00:12:29.715 --> 00:12:33.319
to use your first evaluations,
where you can measure the goal completion

253
00:12:33.552 --> 00:12:37.656
of the agent using answer relevance,
context, relevance, and groundedness.

254
00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:38.858
I'll see you in the next lesson.
